course: dictionary
lesson: 14206
title: Muddled and mixed up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Muddled is an adjective. It describes something mixed up or confused. It comes from the verb «muddle», which means to mix things up. «Sam's sums were muddled, with the steps in the wrong order.» The steps are not in line, so muddled means mixed up and untidy.
explain: Muddled can also describe a person's thinking. «After the long journey, Grandma felt muddled and could not remember what day it was.» Here muddled means confused and not thinking clearly. The words around it are the clue.
explain: Muddled is used in some useful phrases. «We got muddled up in the crowd» means we got mixed up with other people and lost our way. «We managed to muddle through the test» means we coped somehow, even though it was hard.
fact: Muddled means confused and not thinking clearly, or mixed up and in the wrong order.
fact: Muddled is the past form of the verb «muddle», which means to mix things up or confuse them.
fact: In maths, a muddled answer has its steps in the wrong order, so teachers ask for clear working.
fact: Useful phrases: «get muddled», «muddled up», «muddled thinking», and «muddle through», which means manage somehow.
fact: Context helps you choose the meaning: a messy pile of toys means mixed up, but a confused person means muddled in the head.
exercise: «Sam's sums were muddled, with the steps in the wrong order.» What does muddled mean here? | Finished early | Very quiet | *Mixed up and in the wrong order | Neatly written and easy to read
exercise: «After the long journey, Grandma felt muddled and could not remember what day it was.» What does muddled mean here? | *Confused and not thinking clearly | Wet from the rain | Very hungry | Happy and full of energy
exercise: Find and copy the past form of the verb muddle: «The toys were muddled in the box.» | box | the | toys | *muddled
test: «We got muddled up in the crowd and lost our way.» What does muddled up mean? | *Mixed up and separated from others | Tidied away neatly | Standing very still | Singing loudly
test: Ella says, «We will muddle through the test.» What does muddle through mean? | *Manage somehow, even if it is hard | Finish first of everyone | Give up straight away | Cheat on the test
exam: Which phrase means someone is confused in their thinking? | *Muddled thinking | Neat handwriting | Bright sunshine | Quick running shoes
